bring in former counselor to then  president trump kellyanne conway she is  the author of a new book here's the deal  a memoir and it's a great book i'm about  halfway through kellyanne great to see  you thank you brett  there's a lot in this book  about your time in in the trump  administration but also a lot of other  things about politics and and your your  previous history i want to ask you just  about last night and what you've seen so  far in the primaries is there a theme  developing here  on on either side can you kind of paint  a picture for the midterms for  for where we stand  there is brett and i think it's a little  past this prologue in terms of the 2022  midterms this is pretty typical for the  party in power in the first midterm we  know that president clinton suffered 52  seat loss in 1994 president obama  famously referred to his 63 seat loss in  the house in 2010 as a quote shellacking  so one would think that president biden  would  would endorse something similar and we  see  early twinings of that don't we i think  the two major themes coming out of the  primary so far are number one record  enthusiastic turnout in these republican  primaries people feel energized they're  going to the ballot box they're very  upset about what they see with respect  to inflation energy immigration putin  and ukraine and a whole sundry list of  problems in this country that didn't  exist before a short time ago number two  i think what you see in these races is  that  where president trump has endorsed in a  candidate  that candidate is surrounded by other  non-endorsed candidates also running as  america first candidates they want to  somehow hew closely to the policy  accomplishments of president trump and  you're seeing this in very crowded  primaries in pennsylvania which you just  reported on in ohio  in north carolina and the list goes on  and on  i think on the other side brett the  democratic party is it's a fascinating  case study so far in these primaries  they don't have the record turnout that  the republicans have in some of these  states however there's a little bit of a  schism going on you have some  more traditional moderate democrats  running some with president biden's  endorsement and they're going up against  these bernie sanders or elizabeth warren  or aoc or all of the above endorsed  candidates and in some cases the more  radical candidates are winning so i  think you're seeing both parties uh  paying very close attention to these  primaries and we're going to see i  believe a monster year for the  republicans in in the fall typically  that happens but i also think that the  issue set right now does not favor  president biden he doesn't seem to have  any kind of course correction that you  would see from a president whose numbers  are under water we were just talking to  governor camp down in georgia and  obviously those results  seem to be that georgia was ready to  move on from 2020. in this book you say  you were one of the only advisors to  tell  president trump at the time uh that that  he had essentially lost and to accept it  you write in politics 101 you don't tell  the american people what's important to  them they tell you on some days it  seemed the campaign viewed the coveted  pandemic the same way they viewed  hillary or comey or big tech just  another political enemy standing between  trump and reelection and you go on  pretty tough on jared kushner in this  book  repeatedly referred to as the de facto  chief of staff the shadow secretary of  state the real 2020 campaign manager  jarrod had high tailed it to the middle  east just as soon as the campaign brad  and he ran lost the midwest  and the election victory he had so  confidently predicted had not  materialized kind of straight talk  it's straight talk i don't know any  other way and it's all fact checked and  true i'm heartbroken that president  trump didn't win a second term there's a  lot of unfinished business and so many  unraveling of great policies that he put  forth i think that's part of why he's  motivated to seriously consider to run  again brett but the 2020 campaign  had a a certain different tenor tone  and substance in the 2016 campaign in  2016 we were the underdogs  underestimated underfunded under  understaffed and that's a very donald  trump way to be to really turn on its  head this fiction of electability that  pretends i know if you will or won't win  rather than do what we did which is  focus on the electoral colleges which is  electoral college vote which is how you  do or don't win i believe in 2020 they  prove the old adage to be true the  fastest way to make a small fortune is  to have a very large one and waste most  of it 1.4 billion dollars and not much  of it left for ads in those swing states  late in the game  also you know cove had changed the way  president trump was able to campaign  he's a rally guy he likes to go and  connect with the voters it allowed  president biden to sort of hide a little  bit from the voters here now that  benefited him but but i think to run on  policy is the cautionary tale here to  make sure people understand what they  accomplish are and how they affect their  daily lives let me finish with this uh  you said you've told president trump  elections are about the future not the  past  you you talk about the job description  going forward in 2024 and it's not the  who but the what and the strength in the  gop you write for the republican party  lies in its policies and its peoples  and its people voters applaud gop's  commitment to fiscal issues national  security and social issues so that's the  ticket  that's the ticket and uh certainly when  i say elections about the future not the  past let me just say one thing about the  past here we have many voters right now  saying  i  my life was much better not that long  ago and i wanted back so in that regard  they are looking behind them to look  forward a little bit how do we get that  energy independence how do we get the  two dollar gas how do we get border  security how do we get putin out of  ukraine and iran to stop salivating as a  nuclear capable threat to israel and the  list goes on and on but in terms of you  know people are looking at their own  they want a fighter but the question is  are you fighting for yourself or are you  fighting for them and voters usually do  they appreciate people who are fighting  for them that was the magic of trump  2016 and i would say his presidency  brett which which is people felt he was  delivering for the forgotten man  forgotten woman a forgotten child so to  the extent he wants to do that he should  do it is there a trump candidate 2024  he would like to he sees a lot of  unfinished business and like a majority  of voters in everybody's polls brett  people are hurting and they're very  frustrated with the direction that this  white house has taken us into as a  nation on the policies that affect them  every single day so i think those two  things president trump can stand up and  say i had a better economy more border  security putin  china kim jong-un all contained in a way  they're not now and number two um it's i  want to rematch with biden because i can  go to him toe to toe but it whoever runs  if president trump says he's not running  which i believe he would like to whoever  runs is going to have to pick up that  america first mantle because those  policy prescriptions were very popular  and you see the opposite that now in  this current white house but you know  voters don't ask who can win voters ask  who can lead the pundits and the media  we sometimes ask who can who can win who  can win well that was my next question  voters ask who can lead who can win  could he win  well i think the person who can win is  the one who connects with the people and  convincingly talks about policy every  single day not personal grievances not  just the past but the future and the  policy prescriptions that everybody  wants and look i think so long as the  biden harris white house does not do  what president clinton president obama  and so many other leaders have done  which is say you know what this has not  been a great time for the home team  we're going to do a course correction  we're going to admit some mistakes and  we're going to admit that people are  hurting and come out with solutions  they're very reactionary  they talk about gun control after a  tragic unspeakable school shooting they  talk about abortion after the roe league  they talk about putin after he invades  ukraine it's a very reactionary white  house and that's no way i think that  plus spite and unraveling the trump  policies no way to run a country all  right the book is here's the deal  kellyanne conway we appreciate it thanks  hey sean hannity here hey click here to  subscribe to fox news youtube page and  catch our hottest interviews and most  compelling analysis you will not get it  anywhere else

